#X obj 48 98 filledpolygon c 0 1 0 0 w 0 w h 0 h;
#X text 48 118 drawing a rectangle \, interior color 'c' \, border black and one unit thick \, through the points (0 \, 0) \, (w \, 0) \, (w \, h) \, and (0 \, h). Note that the three points containing variables become hot spots for mouse dragging.;
#X text 205 198 Draw the value of 'c' as an Arabic numeral \, at (0 \, -5) coordinates \, in black., f 35;
#X restore 358 919 pd struct03;
#X obj 282 459 pack 0 0 0 0 0, f 29;
#X obj 283 367 trigger bang bang;
#X msg 282 485 append struct03 x \$1 y \$2 w \$3 h \$4 c \$5;
#X text 30 7 Here we introduce the "append message to a [pointer] or [vpointer] object that adds scalars to a window \, and the [set] object that allows you to modify fields of scalars. Our new \, simpler [struct] object has five fields again but merely draws a rectangle and one number for the color. Colors are in '999' form - see below about that., f 86;
#X text 28 72 First an example appending an object: clicking the 'bang' clears the "data03" window and then appends 20 new scalars to it. A [vpointer] object (named with \$0 in order to make sure its name is unique to this patch) is pointed to an empty window (the "clear" message empties it beforehand) using the "traverse" message and then sent 20 "append" messages. Each message specifies random values for the x \, y \, w \, h \, and c fields. This causes a new scalar to be appended after the location the pointer is attached to. Initially this is the head of the (empty) window \, and subsequently the vpointer is updated so that each new scalar is then appended after the previous one., f 86;
#X text 453 250 1: clear the window \, (Another good reason to put your scalars in their own window is so that you can clear the window without clearing the rest of the patch.), f 37;
